{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15735","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15735","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.15735","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyoun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jiyoun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhan","familyName":"Yeo","name":"Yeo, Muhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunhye","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Eunhye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeewon","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jeewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangyul","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Hangyul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Su Ji","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Su Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hee Jo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Hee Jo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hee-Jae","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Hee-Jae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doyun","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Doyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun young","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jun young","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaehun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jung-Oh","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jung-Oh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunjun","familyName":"Kweon","name":"Kweon, Sunjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong Hak","familyName":"Moon","name":"Moon, Jong Hak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daseul","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Daseul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjae","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Minjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Edward","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EHRNote-ChatQA: A Benchmark for Evidence-Grounded Multi-Turn Clinical Question Answering over Longitudinal Discharge Summaries"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-14T10:35:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:55:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T10:33:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-17T00:44:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-21T15:46:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-22T01:18:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Discharge summaries are crucial clinical documents containing the context of a patient's overall hospital stay, and are routinely reviewed by medical experts for patient readmission, ongoing care, and diagnostic decision-making. When reviewing them, medical experts often must iteratively synthesize information across multiple discharge summaries while verifying the evidence supporting each answer. Although large language models (LLMs) are increasingly explored for clinical question answering, existing benchmarks do not sufficiently reflect this setting: they often evaluate exam-style medical knowledge or focus on single-turn question answering with limited evidence-grounding evaluation. We introduce EHRNote-ChatQA, the first benchmark for evidence-grounded multi-turn clinical question answering over patients' multiple discharge summaries. Built from de-identified MIMIC-IV discharge summaries, EHRNote-ChatQA contains 967 patient-level multi-turn samples spanning one to five notes and 16,072 medical-expert-verified QA pairs (8,036 content questions, each paired with an evidence-grounding question) across eight clinical categories. The benchmark is constructed through an expert-informed pipeline combining discharge-summary structuring schema, expert-curated multi-turn QA templates, and LLM-based generation, followed by review and revision of every single QA sample by 12 medical experts (11 physicians and 1 nurse). Benchmarking 24 proprietary and open-weight LLMs reveals several challenges, including that LLMs struggle more with evidence grounding than content answering, multi-turn errors compound across turns, and single-turn clinical QA performance does not reliably transfer to this setting. These findings establish EHRNote-ChatQA as a rigorous and practical benchmark for evaluating clinical QA systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15735","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-16T03:30:11Z","registered":"2026-06-16T03:30:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-22T03:04:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15412","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15412","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.15412","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob","familyName":"Mraz","name":"Mraz, Jakob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomaž","familyName":"Curk","name":"Curk, Tomaž","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Blaž","familyName":"Zupan","name":"Zupan, Blaž","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Few-Shot Biomedical Relation Extraction with Large Language Models: A Viable Alternative to Supervised Learning?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-13T17:37:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:39:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-03T16:48:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-22T01:04:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-41086-3_14","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Biomedical relation extraction (BioRE) is a key step in transforming biomedical literature into structured knowledge. Most existing approaches rely on supervised models trained on costly annotated datasets, limiting their scalability and adaptability across relation types and domains. We investigate few-shot BioRE using prompt-based learning with large language models (LLMs) and compare two task formulations: pairwise classification, which predicts relations for individual entity pairs, and joint generation, which extracts multiple relations in a single model call. Experiments on the BioREDirect dataset reveal a clear precision-recall trade-off. Pairwise classification achieves higher recall, whereas joint generation is more precise and computationally efficient. The best-performing model achieves a micro-F1 score of 0.44, substantially outperforming previous few-shot results (0.34) while remaining below the supervised baseline (0.56). Much of this gap is attributable to a single ambiguously defined relation type. When evaluated using macro-F1, which better captures performance across relation types in an imbalanced setting, prompt-based approaches outperform the supervised baseline (0.45 vs. 0.38), particularly on rare relation types. These findings highlight the potential of LLMs for BioRE in low-resource settings and underscore the importance of well-defined relation schemas."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15412","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-16T03:23:13Z","registered":"2026-06-16T03:23:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-22T03:04:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15569","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15569","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.15569","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomoya","familyName":"Wakayama","name":"Wakayama, Tomoya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Decision-Theoretic View of Test-Time Training: When, How Far, and Which Directions to Adapt"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-14T03:16:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:08:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-19T14:54:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T02:31:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time training (TTT) adapts a pretrained model to each prompt via parameter updates, improving accuracy under pretraining-to-test distribution shifts. Yet, its performance often suffers from instability and sensitivity to hyperparameters such as update steps and subspace. We explain this behavior through a decision-theoretic lens, treating TTT as implicit Bayesian inference in the kernel regime. Under a Gaussian process benchmark, we show that TTT reduces prediction error when updates are spectrally matched to the prompt's signal-to-noise ratio and aligned with query-relevant eigen-directions. This perspective underpins the following results: (1) we show when fixed update steps and subspaces fail under distribution shifts, motivating adaptive strategies; (2) we prove that selecting update steps via prompt evidence admits a PAC-Bayes guarantee against overfitting; and (3) we characterize the Bayes-optimal update subspace under a linear-Gaussian correction model, yielding a scoring rule for selecting Transformer blocks and heads. Our theory helps explain the empirical instability of TTT, taking a step toward principled guidance for when, how far, and which directions to adapt."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15569","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-16T03:26:39Z","registered":"2026-06-16T03:26:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:25:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengtao","familyName":"Gui","name":"Gui, Zhengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouxing","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhouxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating the Robustness of Proof Autoformalization in Lean 4"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T18:10:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:05:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-16T19:24:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T00:10:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Proof autoformalization aims to translate a mathematical informal proof in natural language into a formal proof in a formal language such as Lean 4. Existing LLM-based models for proof autoformalization are typically evaluated only on well-formed informal proofs from curated datasets. We argue that a robust proof autoformalizer must remain faithful even for informal proofs that diverge from these idealized ones, and we present the first study on the robustness of proof autoformalization models. We formulate two categories of perturbations: a global perturbation paraphrases the informal theorem-proof pair in a different style, under which the formalization should remain consistent; a local perturbation alters a value, symbol, or proof step, possibly in a counterfactual way, and a robust formalization should faithfully reflect the perturbation rather than reverting to the original or inferring a different one on its own. We build a benchmark with both perturbations on miniF2F and MATH-500, and automatically measure how stable a proof autoformalization's correctness is under global perturbations and how faithfully its output reflects local perturbations. We evaluate seven recent models: their correctness varies under global perturbations, and they mostly fail to remain faithful under local perturbations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-16T03:11:16Z","registered":"2026-06-16T03:11:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:24:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15734","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15734","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.15734","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihang","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Weihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jingyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyao","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Qingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianming","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Jianming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bangde","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Bangde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Retrievable Gradients: Continual Post-Training Without Cumulative Weight Drift"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-14T10:31:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:55:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-11T15:04:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T01:31:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continual post-training enables models to absorb emerging knowledge after deployment, but repeatedly updating shared parameters can accumulate weight drift, potentially causing catastrophic forgetting and degrading general capabilities. Retrieval-augmented generation avoids such parameter drift, yet often lacks the depth of parametric knowledge integration. In this paper, we propose ReGrad (Retrievable Gradients), a new paradigm that treats gradients as retrievable units of knowledge. ReGrad pre-computes document-specific gradients offline, stores them in an indexed Gradient Bank, and retrieves only query-relevant gradients at inference time for temporary weight adaptation. However, raw language-modeling gradients are optimized for token-level document reconstruction rather than for query-driven knowledge use. We therefore introduce a bi-level meta-learning objective that reshapes document-derived gradients into generalizable adaptation signals for downstream tasks. Experiments across general and domain-specific settings show that \\textsc{ReGrad} outperforms CPT and RAG baselines, enabling scalable and reversible parametric knowledge injection without accumulating weight drift."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15734","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-16T03:30:10Z","registered":"2026-06-16T03:30:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15610","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15610","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.15610","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroyasu","familyName":"Usami","name":"Usami, Hiroyasu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keisuke","familyName":"Hara","name":"Hara, Keisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayato","familyName":"Tsuboi","name":"Tsuboi, Ayato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naohiko","familyName":"Matsuda","name":"Matsuda, Naohiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Judges Have Dark Current: Stratified Null and Utility Tests for LLM Judge Reliability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-14T05:34:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:50:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-12T20:24:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:58:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Passing an exact-clone null gate does not entail passing a matched target-selection utility gate. We call a non-tie preference between exact duplicates dark current. On 102 prespecified fresh RewardBench 2 rows, all four configuration--criterion cells report 0/102 clone preferences. Both neutral utility arms pass, but strict utility lower bounds of .539 and .745 miss the .75 working threshold. Conversely, re-evaluation of the same rows on two additional configurations yields a neutral gpt-oss utility pass with 2/102 clone preferences, leaving low-null behavior uncertified. A separately frozen 485-row extension passes both aggregate working gates under both criteria on one local raw-text configuration, yet strict Precise Instruction Following (Precise IF) alignment is 58/154 (.377). Thus matched null and utility results can diverge, and aggregate passage can coexist with weak subset alignment. Interpretation is conditional on the declared configurations, fixed source mixtures, source-label targets, and study-specific working models. The bounds are marginal, the subset rate is descriptive, and non-certification does not prove a defect."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 4 figures, 9 tables. Substantially revised: input-stratified null and target-selection utility audits, additional fixed-configuration studies, and an operational audit protocol. Supersedes v1, originally titled \"LLM Judges Have Dark Current: A Psychometric Datasheet for LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluation.\""}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15610","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-16T03:27:32Z","registered":"2026-06-16T03:27:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:37:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15007","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.15007","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.15007","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Allan"],"familyName":"NVIDIA","name":"NVIDIA","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Allan"],"familyName":":","name":":","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Allan"],"givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Blakeman","name":"Blakeman, Aaron","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Allan"],"givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Aaron","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Allan"],"givenName":"Aastha","familyName":"Jhunjhunwala","name":"Jhunjhunwala, Aastha","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Allan"],"givenName":"Abhibha","familyName":"Gupta","na